---
source: tests/cli_help.rs
expression: help_output(args)
---
Show, list or switch the context: a named set of lists that narrows the everyday reads

Contexts are defined in config.toml as `[contexts.<name>]` with `folders`, `lists` and `default_list`. The active one narrows `tasks list`, search, `next`, `waiting`, My Day's suggestions and the TUI to its lists, and a new task with no list goes to its default list. An explicit --list always wins; My Day itself is never narrowed

Usage: ms-todo ctx [OPTIONS] [NAME]
       ms-todo ctx <COMMAND>

Commands:
  show  The active context and the lists it covers (the same as `ctx`)
  list  Every context config.toml defines, with the lists each covers now
  help  Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [NAME]
          Make this context active (as named in config.toml), or `none` to show everything again

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: jsonl is one object per line, ids one ID per line, csv has a header row [default: table in a terminal, json when piped]

          [possible values: table, json, jsonl, ids, csv]

      --instance <NAME>
          Use a separate copy of ms-todo's data (also MS_TODO_INSTANCE). Builds run from Cargo's target/ directory default to "dev"; "default" is the installed copy

      --fresh
          Before reading, sync and wait, so the answer is as fresh as Microsoft To Do's (a read is otherwise answered from the cache)

      --quiet
          Print nothing on stderr but errors: no notes and no progress

      --no-color
          No colour or bold in tables (also NO_COLOR)

      --context <NAME|none>
          Read this command in this context (from config.toml) rather than the active one; `none` for every list

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
